1
00:00:00,258 --> 00:00:04,862
Intravascular Ultrasound, or
IVUS: Setup and Coronary Imaging,

2
00:00:04,912 --> 00:00:09,508
explained by Dr. Mohd Maqbool
Sohil, Interventional Cardiologist.

3
00:00:09,891 --> 00:00:15,603
The IVUS system: an imaging console connects
by cable to a patient interface module,

4
00:00:15,653 --> 00:00:20,296
or PIM, on a motorised pullback
sled, covered with a sterile bag.

5
00:00:20,691 --> 00:00:23,191
The catheter is connected to the PIM.

6
00:00:23,772 --> 00:00:28,460
Rotational catheters are flushed with
heparinised saline to remove air,

7
00:00:28,510 --> 00:00:30,296
which would degrade the image.

8
00:00:30,791 --> 00:00:35,253
Rotational catheters, 40–60
MHz, high definition,

9
00:00:35,303 --> 00:00:39,470
or phased-array solid-state
catheters, of about 20 MHz.

10
00:00:40,039 --> 00:00:44,753
They are about 3 Fr, compatible
with a 6 Fr guide catheter.

11
00:00:45,224 --> 00:00:50,317
Before instrumenting the coronary artery:
systemic heparin with ACT monitoring,

12
00:00:50,367 --> 00:00:53,569
and intracoronary nitroglycerin
to prevent spasm.

13
00:00:53,938 --> 00:00:54,878
Step one.

14
00:00:55,459 --> 00:00:57,507
The guide catheter is engaged,

15
00:00:57,638 --> 00:01:03,733
and a 0.014-inch guidewire is advanced
across the lesion to the distal vessel.

16
00:01:04,071 --> 00:01:04,880
Step two.

17
00:01:05,509 --> 00:01:09,376
The IVUS catheter is advanced
over the wire, monorail,

18
00:01:09,426 --> 00:01:15,354
until the transducer lies well beyond the
lesion, ideally more than 10 mm distal.

19
00:01:15,704 --> 00:01:16,645
Step three.

20
00:01:17,226 --> 00:01:21,083
Automated pullback at about 0.5–1 mm/s:

21
00:01:21,142 --> 00:01:26,069
the sled draws the transducer back while
a live short-axis image is acquired,

22
00:01:26,119 --> 00:01:27,440
shown time-compressed.

23
00:01:27,871 --> 00:01:30,073
Stacked frames form the L-mode,

24
00:01:30,252 --> 00:01:32,595
a longitudinal view used to locate the

25
00:01:32,645 --> 00:01:35,609
minimal lumen site and
the reference segments.

26
00:01:35,984 --> 00:01:39,853
Short-axis anatomy: the
lumen, intima and plaque,

27
00:01:39,937 --> 00:01:42,518
the dark media, the bright adventitia,

28
00:01:42,568 --> 00:01:48,353
and the EEM border, with artefacts from
the guidewire and catheter ring-down.

29
00:01:48,671 --> 00:01:52,050
Plaque types: fibrous,
with intermediate echoes;

30
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:55,335
attenuated, with signal
loss without calcium;

31
00:01:55,385 --> 00:02:00,183
and calcified, bright with shadowing,
with the arc measured in degrees.

32
00:02:00,624 --> 00:02:07,612
At the minimal lumen area, or MLA, site,
the lumen and EEM borders are traced.

33
00:02:08,205 --> 00:02:15,146
Plaque burden is the EEM area minus the
lumen area, divided by the EEM area.

34
00:02:15,538 --> 00:02:21,002
Proximal and distal references guide
sizing, EEM-based or lumen-based.

35
00:02:21,595 --> 00:02:27,488
Landing zones with plaque burden < 50%
define the stent length on L-mode.

36
00:02:28,181 --> 00:02:33,443
EEM-based sizing usually allows a
larger stent than lumen-based sizing.

37
00:02:34,024 --> 00:02:38,441
Heavily calcified segments need
lesion preparation before stenting.

38
00:02:38,824 --> 00:02:43,396
After stenting, IVUS checks
minimum stent area, or MSA,

39
00:02:43,479 --> 00:02:46,836
apposition, edge dissection
and tissue protrusion.

40
00:02:47,417 --> 00:02:50,679
Here, the stent is
underexpanded in calcium.

41
00:02:51,058 --> 00:02:56,046
Non-compliant balloon post-dilatation
at high pressure improves expansion.

42
00:02:56,639 --> 00:03:03,877
The MSA rises above 5.5 mm², and
above 90% of the distal reference.

43
00:03:04,251 --> 00:03:10,141
Final run: the stent is well expanded and
apposed, with no major edge dissection,

44
00:03:10,191 --> 00:03:14,632
and landing zones in segments
with plaque burden < 50%.

45
00:03:15,051 --> 00:03:15,956
Key points.

46
00:03:16,560 --> 00:03:17,522
Why IVUS:

47
00:03:17,572 --> 00:03:21,475
randomised trials and
meta-analyses show fewer adverse

48
00:03:21,525 --> 00:03:25,165
events with IVUS-guided than
angiography-guided stenting,

49
00:03:25,215 --> 00:03:29,263
and it measures vessel size,
plaque and the stent result.

50
00:03:29,858 --> 00:03:34,760
Indications include left main
disease, long and calcified lesions,

51
00:03:34,810 --> 00:03:40,215
chronic total occlusion, bifurcation,
stent failure, and ambiguous angiography.

52
00:03:40,624 --> 00:03:45,803
This animation is for education and is
not a substitute for medical advice.

